WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.672
This briefing comes from Book 05 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.672 --> 00:00:07.830
Devices and Endpoints:
the move from Group Policy

3
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.988
to Intune for devices, and
to Azure Arc for servers.

4
00:00:10.988 --> 00:00:12.995
I'll take it through four questions:

5
00:00:12.995 --> 00:00:16.940
why Group Policy can't simply be
carried to the cloud, what replaces it,

6
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.124
who owns the endpoint, and
how to migrate a fleet without

7
00:00:20.124 --> 00:00:23.238
losing the governance Group
Policy quietly provided.

8
00:00:23.238 --> 00:00:26.213
The short version: this is
not a settings migration.

9
00:00:26.213 --> 00:00:28.140
It's a governance rebuild.

10
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.078
Why can't Group Policy come to the cloud?

11
00:00:31.078 --> 00:00:33.746
Because Group Policy governed by position,

12
00:00:33.746 --> 00:00:36.489
and an Intune-managed
device has no position.

13
00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:40.490
Where a computer sat in the OU
tree quietly did five jobs.

14
00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.753
It decided targeting: what
settings the computer got.

15
00:00:43.753 --> 00:00:48.027
Precedence: link order and
inheritance decided which setting won.

16
00:00:48.027 --> 00:00:51.541
Delegation: OU permissions
decided who could change it.

17
00:00:51.541 --> 00:00:56.115
Timing: policy refreshed at
startup, at sign-in and on a cycle.

18
00:00:56.115 --> 00:01:00.402
And evidence: the resultant set of
policy told you what actually applied.

19
00:01:00.402 --> 00:01:03.864
An Entra-joined, Intune-managed
device has none of that.

20
00:01:03.864 --> 00:01:08.440
Policy is assigned flatly to groups,
and nothing reads the OU tree.

21
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.877
When two policies set the same thing,

22
00:01:10.877 --> 00:01:14.615
Intune reports a conflict; it
doesn't resolve it by order.

23
00:01:14.615 --> 00:01:17.128
Devices check in about every eight hours.

24
00:01:17.128 --> 00:01:20.443
And some Group Policy jobs,
mapped drives for example,

25
00:01:20.443 --> 00:01:22.553
have no built-in equivalent at all.

26
00:01:22.553 --> 00:01:26.977
Microsoft itself advises against
copying GPOs one for one.

27
00:01:26.977 --> 00:01:29.003
So this is not a settings migration.

28
00:01:29.003 --> 00:01:32.669
Teams that copy GPOs into
Intune discover the governance

29
00:01:32.669 --> 00:01:35.953
loss after the targeting
model has already changed.

30
00:01:35.953 --> 00:01:40.400
Every job the tree did implicitly
has to be rebuilt explicitly.

31
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.925
The obvious shortcut is hybrid join,
and the book calls it a trap, carefully.

32
00:01:45.925 --> 00:01:48.162
Microsoft's guidance has two halves.

33
00:01:48.162 --> 00:01:51.036
New, refurbished or reset Windows devices

34
00:01:51.036 --> 00:01:54.074
should be Microsoft
Entra joined by default.

35
00:01:54.074 --> 00:01:57.461
Existing domain-joined
devices can be hybrid joined,

36
00:01:57.461 --> 00:02:01.463
which gives them a cloud identity
with minimal impact on users.

37
00:02:01.463 --> 00:02:06.538
But Microsoft is explicit that hybrid join
shouldn't be the long-term end state.

38
00:02:06.538 --> 00:02:07.613
Here's the trap.

39
00:02:07.613 --> 00:02:10.838
Turning on hybrid join for
a fleet is, mechanically,

40
00:02:10.838 --> 00:02:15.062
close to a single tenant-wide
switch, so a control that assessments

41
00:02:15.062 --> 00:02:18.825
score as a multi-quarter program
can look finished in a week.

42
00:02:18.825 --> 00:02:22.300
But the fleet keeps every
on-premises dependency it had.

43
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.388
And there's no Microsoft-supported
way to convert a

44
00:02:25.388 --> 00:02:29.212
hybrid-joined device to Entra
join without resetting Windows.

45
00:02:29.212 --> 00:02:32.637
So the governed path uses
hybrid join where it helps,

46
00:02:32.637 --> 00:02:36.540
and plans each device's move to
Entra join at a natural event:

47
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.762
the next hardware refresh,
operating-system upgrade or rebuild.

48
00:02:40.762 --> 00:02:44.570
That way the waypoint doesn't
quietly become the destination.

49
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.483
What replaces it?

50
00:02:46.483 --> 00:02:49.057
Devices and servers take different roads.

51
00:02:49.057 --> 00:02:53.013
For endpoints, the Entra ID device
object carries the identity,

52
00:02:53.013 --> 00:02:57.956
Intune carries configuration and
compliance, Autopilot provides zero-touch

53
00:02:57.956 --> 00:03:02.671
provisioning, and Defender for Endpoint
covers threat and vulnerability.

54
00:03:02.671 --> 00:03:06.433
Servers are different, and the old
design documents got this wrong.

55
00:03:06.433 --> 00:03:08.895
Intune doesn't manage Windows Server.

56
00:03:08.895 --> 00:03:13.720
A server comes under governance through
Azure Arc, which makes it an Azure resource.

57
00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:17.312
Azure Policy machine configuration
sets its configuration,

58
00:03:17.312 --> 00:03:21.820
and Update Manager and Defender for
Servers handle patching and protection.

59
00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:27.208
For new devices, the book's doctrine is
Intune-first: governed from day zero.

60
00:03:27.208 --> 00:03:31.226
The organizational assignment is
set when the device is ordered,

61
00:03:31.226 --> 00:03:35.616
enrollment is zero-touch, and an
onboarding failure goes to quarantine,

62
00:03:35.616 --> 00:03:38.220
never into production in a degraded state.

63
00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.397
Because each system is
authoritative for one thing,

64
00:03:41.397 --> 00:03:45.904
the program correlates them on stable
keys and reconciles the inventory

65
00:03:45.904 --> 00:03:50.920
continuously, so the fleet has one answer
to "what do we have, and is it governed?"

66
00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:56.883
For new devices, the book's doctrine is
Intune-first, and it has five pillars.

67
00:03:56.883 --> 00:03:59.783
One: no ungoverned device, ever.

68
00:03:59.783 --> 00:04:04.202
Every device is enrolled in governance
before it goes into production use,

69
00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:07.482
and quarantine is the safety
valve, not the steady state.

70
00:04:07.482 --> 00:04:10.720
Two: procurement is the
first governance step.

71
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.626
The organizational assignment is
set when the order is placed,

72
00:04:14.626 --> 00:04:18.608
not when the box arrives, the same
way HR feeds user provisioning.

73
00:04:18.608 --> 00:04:20.583
Three: zero-touch only.

74
00:04:20.583 --> 00:04:24.915
Manual enrollment is an exception
that needs written justification,

75
00:04:24.915 --> 00:04:26.782
a time limit and an audit entry.

76
00:04:26.782 --> 00:04:29.296
Four: one management plane per asset.

77
00:04:29.296 --> 00:04:34.086
Intune for endpoints, Azure Arc with
machine configuration for servers,

78
00:04:34.086 --> 00:04:37.120
and no greenfield Group
Policy or hybrid join.

79
00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.633
And five: quarantine on failure.

80
00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:41.887
An onboarding failure opens a ticket;

81
00:04:41.887 --> 00:04:45.158
it never produces a device
running in a degraded state.

82
00:04:45.158 --> 00:04:50.227
One practical choice: in a GCC tenant,
the book defaults to classic Autopilot,

83
00:04:50.227 --> 00:04:54.089
with devices registered by
the manufacturer or reseller,

84
00:04:54.089 --> 00:04:57.308
because that registration
can carry a group tag.

85
00:04:57.308 --> 00:05:02.258
The newer Autopilot device preparation
has no registration step, so it can't.

86
00:05:02.258 --> 00:05:05.533
These pillars are program
doctrine for net-new devices.

87
00:05:05.533 --> 00:05:08.650
The existing fleet takes
one of three paths.

88
00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.321
There are three paths to
the same governed state,

89
00:05:12.321 --> 00:05:14.512
and the book uses them deliberately.

90
00:05:14.512 --> 00:05:17.452
The governed path is program-orchestrated:

91
00:05:17.452 --> 00:05:20.637
every step is recorded,
approved and evidenced.

92
00:05:20.637 --> 00:05:23.575
It's the default for
any population in scope.

93
00:05:23.575 --> 00:05:26.699
The manual path is operator-run
with the native tools,

94
00:05:26.699 --> 00:05:28.900
without the orchestration platform.

95
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.455
It's for when the platform
isn't available yet,

96
00:05:31.455 --> 00:05:34.838
and it trades automation for
more hand-collected evidence.

97
00:05:34.838 --> 00:05:39.155
The retrofit path brings an estate
that was already modernized by hand,

98
00:05:39.155 --> 00:05:42.813
before the program existed,
under governance after the fact.

99
00:05:42.813 --> 00:05:44.987
All three end in the same place:

100
00:05:44.987 --> 00:05:49.075
the device or server enrolled,
baselined and reconciled.

101
00:05:49.075 --> 00:05:51.810
What differs is how much
of the proof is produced

102
00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:54.887
automatically and how much
has to be collected by hand.

103
00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:59.476
The chapter also corrects a few
things the old path guides got wrong,

104
00:05:59.476 --> 00:06:04.309
for example that LAN Manager
authentication level five still accepts

105
00:06:04.309 --> 00:06:09.750
NTLM version two; blocking NTLM takes
the separate Restrict NTLM policies.

106
00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.788
Who owns the endpoint?

107
00:06:11.788 --> 00:06:14.662
The book separates who decides
from who does the work.

108
00:06:14.662 --> 00:06:21.163
Deciding: the CISO and ISSO own the
baselines, the exceptions and the evidence.

109
00:06:21.163 --> 00:06:25.163
The endpoint engineering lead owns
policy design and the rollout.

110
00:06:25.163 --> 00:06:30.426
And the change board owns the update
rings, the migration waves and any pause.

111
00:06:30.426 --> 00:06:34.284
Doing: endpoint engineering
builds the Intune policies,

112
00:06:34.284 --> 00:06:37.075
Autopilot profiles and app deployments.

113
00:06:37.075 --> 00:06:41.963
The server team onboards servers to
Azure Arc and owns machine configuration.

114
00:06:41.963 --> 00:06:46.475
Security operations runs Defender,
the alerts and the response.

115
00:06:46.475 --> 00:06:52.137
Consulted and affected: application owners
supply test results and app packaging.

116
00:06:52.137 --> 00:06:55.551
The help desk handles
enrollment, wipe and recovery.

117
00:06:55.551 --> 00:06:58.825
And end users join pilot
waves and tell us what broke.

118
00:06:58.825 --> 00:07:03.325
One rule applies to the people as
much as the devices: least privilege.

119
00:07:03.325 --> 00:07:07.647
Day-to-day work runs on scoped Intune
roles, not Intune Administrator;

120
00:07:07.647 --> 00:07:12.860
Microsoft advises against using the
broad role for routine administration.

121
00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:14.223
How.

122
00:07:14.223 --> 00:07:19.358
The book runs the Group Policy sunset
as a program, setting by setting,

123
00:07:19.358 --> 00:07:24.674
in seven steps: inventory and parse,
classify each setting, crosswalk it,

124
00:07:24.674 --> 00:07:29.810
decide, sequence by population, freeze
and unlink, and finally retire.

125
00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:33.546
Every setting goes one of three
ways: migrate it to Intune,

126
00:07:33.546 --> 00:07:36.885
replace it with a cloud-native
pattern, or retire it.

127
00:07:36.885 --> 00:07:40.585
The coexistence rules matter, and
the old guides had them wrong.

128
00:07:40.585 --> 00:07:44.710
While a device is under both,
Group Policy wins by default.

129
00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:47.286
The MDMWinsOverGP setting reverses that,

130
00:07:47.286 --> 00:07:51.480
but only for settings in the Policy
configuration service provider;

131
00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.571
Defender settings and
Windows Hello for Business

132
00:07:54.571 --> 00:07:57.735
have to be removed from
Group Policy, not overridden.

133
00:07:57.735 --> 00:08:02.999
Group Policy preferences can leave their
settings behind after a GPO is unlinked,

134
00:08:02.999 --> 00:08:06.173
so every preference item
needs a planned cleanup.

135
00:08:06.173 --> 00:08:10.825
And the book unlinks one population at
a time rather than blocking inheritance,

136
00:08:10.825 --> 00:08:13.010
because that's scoped and reversible.

137
00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.747
One more thing: measure, don't assume.

138
00:08:15.747 --> 00:08:19.990
Microsoft publishes no typical
percentage of settings that migrate,

139
00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:24.950
so the program runs Group Policy analytics
against the estate's own exports.

140
00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:28.693
The existing fleet then
migrates in seven phases,

141
00:08:28.693 --> 00:08:32.125
about thirty-four weeks on
the book's planning estimates.

142
00:08:32.125 --> 00:08:34.553
Phase one inventories and classifies,

143
00:08:34.553 --> 00:08:37.588
ending with a decision
record for every setting.

144
00:08:37.588 --> 00:08:42.513
Phase two plans coexistence, with
pilot devices co-managed and syncing.

145
00:08:42.513 --> 00:08:45.793
Phase three authors the
baselines, unassigned,

146
00:08:45.793 --> 00:08:49.325
until every setting is
owned by exactly one policy.

147
00:08:49.325 --> 00:08:52.588
Phase four pilots on about
five percent of devices.

148
00:08:52.588 --> 00:08:56.268
Phase five cuts over workload
by workload in four waves,

149
00:08:56.268 --> 00:08:58.588
each watched for seventy-two hours.

150
00:08:58.588 --> 00:09:03.976
Phase six verifies that no migrated
device still processes a GPO.

151
00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:06.069
And phase seven retires the GPOs,

152
00:09:06.069 --> 00:09:10.025
keeping them ninety days before
they're archived and deleted.

153
00:09:10.025 --> 00:09:14.775
The book also sets a floor:
Windows 11 version 24H2.

154
00:09:14.775 --> 00:09:18.843
Windows 10 is out of
support, and Windows 11 23H2

155
00:09:18.843 --> 00:09:23.537
Enterprise stops getting
updates on November 10, 2026.

156
00:09:23.537 --> 00:09:26.572
These are planning estimates
for a large fleet with

157
00:09:26.572 --> 00:09:29.888
an existing Configuration
Manager estate, not promises.

158
00:09:29.888 --> 00:09:32.667
Every gate is the next
phase's precondition,

159
00:09:32.667 --> 00:09:35.890
and every phase has a
rollback while it's open.

160
00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.565
Servers come under governance
through Azure Arc.

161
00:09:39.565 --> 00:09:43.442
Onboarding uses the Connected
Machine agent with least privilege:

162
00:09:43.442 --> 00:09:47.803
a certificate-based onboarding identity
that's needed only at connect time.

163
00:09:47.803 --> 00:09:50.907
Configuration comes through
Azure Policy machine

164
00:09:50.907 --> 00:09:53.578
configuration baselines,
assigned by scope.

165
00:09:53.578 --> 00:09:58.190
And patching and protection come from
Update Manager and Defender for Servers.

166
00:09:58.190 --> 00:09:59.853
Two dates make this urgent.

167
00:09:59.853 --> 00:10:07.687
Extended security updates for Windows Server
2012 and 2012 R2 ended on October 13,

168
00:10:07.687 --> 00:10:13.102
2026, a week ago, so any remaining
2012 host is a red finding.

169
00:10:13.102 --> 00:10:19.453
And Windows Server 2016 reaches
end of support on January 12, 2027.

170
00:10:19.453 --> 00:10:21.762
The book is careful about rollback too:

171
00:10:21.762 --> 00:10:26.309
remove extensions before disconnecting
the agent, because disconnecting

172
00:10:26.309 --> 00:10:31.010
deletes the Azure resource, which the
onboarding role isn't allowed to do.

173
00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:33.860
Finally, run it and prove it.

174
00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:38.647
In run-state, compliance feeds
Conditional Access, which is Book 04.

175
00:10:38.647 --> 00:10:41.999
Updates flow through rings
and Windows Autopatch,

176
00:10:41.999 --> 00:10:47.265
and in a GCC tenant the update evidence
comes from Intune and Autopatch reports,

177
00:10:47.265 --> 00:10:51.973
because Windows Update for Business
reports don't meet GCC compliance.

178
00:10:51.973 --> 00:10:58.910
The inventory is reconciled across Entra
ID, Intune, Autopilot, Arc and the CMDB.

179
00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:02.785
Devices are retired and wiped
before the account is deleted.

180
00:11:02.785 --> 00:11:09.414
And the evidence maps to the
configuration-management and

181
00:11:09.414 --> 00:11:14.960
flaw-remediation controls:
CM-2, CM-6, CM-7, CM-8 and SI-2.

182
00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:17.498
What we need from
leadership is four things.

183
00:11:17.498 --> 00:11:20.411
Approve Intune-first for every new device.

184
00:11:20.411 --> 00:11:24.697
Fund the Group Policy inventory
and the Group Policy analytics run.

185
00:11:24.697 --> 00:11:30.397
Set the Windows 11 24H2 floor and a plan
for servers reaching end of support.

186
00:11:30.397 --> 00:11:34.210
And name the owners for
endpoints, servers and security.

187
00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:39.510
Book 05 has the full migration, with
every external claim footnoted.

188
00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:43.387
Book 04 covers what a
compliant device may reach,

189
00:11:43.387 --> 00:11:46.198
and Book 02 the identity it rests on.

190
00:11:46.198 --> 00:11:47.020
Thank you.
